00:12:55.960 --> 00:13:03.049
Parisa Ataei: I'm moving on to… I'm sorry, Jesus. To report on numerate subtyping.

125
00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:12.569
Kevin Millikin: Yep. So, this is a story that leads to a kind of a big breaking change that we might make in the next release.

126
00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:23.770
Kevin Millikin: We are adding a, a new type to Compact. It's called JubJub Scalar, and it's the type of the, of the scalar field of the JubJub curve.

127
00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:41.259
Kevin Millikin: The… we have a eCMAL function for a jump-jump curve, and we have an ECMAL generator function. They take a field-type argument, and this isn't really correct, because, not all field values would… would technically be things you should pass there.

128
00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:46.880
Kevin Millikin: The JubJub field is a different field with a smaller modulus than the native field.

129
00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:57.450
Kevin Millikin: So, so we… so the suggestion was, let's add an explicit job dubbscaler type, and let's implement the subtyping rules for… for that.

130
00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:08.680
Kevin Millikin: The status quo is that we have, right now, field, and we have a whole hierarchy of Uint types, subtype, a subtype hierarchy, and we have field at the top.

131
00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:11.969
Kevin Millikin: And where does JubJub Scalar fit?

132
00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:19.300
Kevin Millikin: So, answer number one is that there is a JubJub field modulus, and it fits at that point in the hierarchy.

133
00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:29.169
Kevin Millikin: So all the UINCH types that are smaller, where the upper bound is smaller than that field modulus are subtypes, and all the ones that are larger are supertypes.

134
00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:35.470
Kevin Millikin: This is not horrible, from a technical standpoint.

135
00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:53.329
Kevin Millikin: it leads to a kind of interesting question, which is that the least upper bounds of pairs of numeric types no longer exist, because the least upper bound of JubJubScaler and the Uint type that has exactly that maximum value of the field modulus.

136
00:14:53.850 --> 00:15:00.969
Kevin Millikin: is, or 1 minus the field modulus. They're, mutual subtypes, but they're not the same type.

137
00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:09.030
Kevin Millikin: And this is kind of minor, actually, I think. You could resolve this by just saying, by…

138
00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:16.409
Kevin Millikin: We declare that when you get a lease upper bound, that you get these two… these two types.

139
00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:21.069
Kevin Millikin: The… the result is one or the other of them that we prefer.

140
00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:26.890
Kevin Millikin: However, the crypto team does not like this idea.

141
00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:36.610
Kevin Millikin: it offends their intuition, and I think we should listen to that, to them about that. And then they've also pointed out that not every…

142
00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:42.139
Kevin Millikin: Foreign field type that we will add is a, integer modulo sum.

143
00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:43.450
Kevin Millikin: Field modulus.

144
00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:49.859
Kevin Millikin: Sweet. So, this doesn't tell us what we would do with those kinds of exotic foreign fields.

145
00:15:50.220 --> 00:16:07.779
Kevin Millikin: The second idea that we had was that the… we could look at a type like JubJubScaler or the native field type as having infinitely many values. Any integer value is a member of that type. And they're just kind of canonicalized as performing the modular reduction.

146
00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:25.370
Kevin Millikin: So then, JubJubScaler in that world would be another top type of the, of the unsigned integer hierarchy. It would be different from the field type. We'd have the same problem with least upper bounds that we would resolve by preferring one or the other.

147
00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:36.629
Kevin Millikin: This has a kind of unfortunate consequence. We would have… we would have implicit upcasts from unsigned integer types to JubJubScaler.

148
00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:56.140
Kevin Millikin: Some of those upcasts would require code at runtime to perform the modular reduction, and this is something that we desperately don't want to take into the language from… in my viewpoint, because it has, it has knock-on consequences. So one of them is that we have vector subtyping.

149
00:16:56.510 --> 00:17:02.649
Kevin Millikin: Which means if I pass a vector of an unsigned integer type where I expect a vector of JubJubScaler.

150
00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:07.330
Kevin Millikin: I potentially have to convert all of the elements of the vector.

151
00:17:07.609 --> 00:17:10.369
Kevin Millikin: And that can be expensive, and I'll have to do that in circuit as well.

152
00:17:11.349 --> 00:17:23.019
Kevin Millikin: And then if we ever introduce, higher order circuits or witnesses, we have a similar problem. If I have a… we'll have the obvious subtyping for arrow types.

153
00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:26.820
Kevin Millikin: If I pass a subtype of a… of a…

154
00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:38.710
Kevin Millikin: first-class circuit or witness, as a super… as a value of a supertype, I have to wrap that value to convert the inputs when I invoke it, and to convert… convert the output when I get it.

155
00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:52.419
Kevin Millikin: And this is also to be avoided, because what experience has taught us is that you'll end up with long chains of wrapped functions that are all doing conversions. And you can't… you can't really eliminate them statically.

156
00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:55.330
Kevin Millikin: So, we should avoid that, I think.

157
00:17:56.150 --> 00:18:10.220
Kevin Millikin: So the… so that's kind of an awkward one. I think you could avoid this. You could say that you actually represent the job-jub scalars as non-canonic representations, and you just have to canonicalize them when you perform arithmetic or equality comparisons.

158
00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:14.299
Kevin Millikin: It just shifts the burden from the cat-type cats to somewhere else.

159
00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:26.479
Kevin Millikin: The third thing that we thought of, and this is what I've implemented, is that JubJubScaler is unrelated by subtyping to all of the unsigned integer types and to field.

160
00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:30.559
Kevin Millikin: And then if we do that, we should do it for field as well.

161
00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:39.690
Kevin Millikin: And that is a major breaking change. So the new… so the change would be that, field is no longer a supertype of all of the unsigned integer types.

162
00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:58.579
Kevin Millikin: And this is actually pretty good, because we have the idea that when we add big ints, we want them to just seamlessly be part of the unsigned integer type hierarchy. And so field would be slotted right in the middle of that, and we have exactly the same problem with field, then, that we have with JubJubScaler now.

163
00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.440
Kevin Millikin: So… split, so…

164
00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:15.180
Kevin Millikin: the TLDR, splitting field out from unsigned integer, it's no longer a supertype of all the unsigned integer types. Consequence of this is that you have to add explicit casts to field in lots of places.

165
00:19:18.650 --> 00:19:19.749
Kevin Millikin: Go ahead, Jonathan.

166
00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:27.559
Jonathan Sobel: I was just gonna say that when I very, very, very first learned Compact and was working on the first tutorial and everything.

167
00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:32.360
Jonathan Sobel: I found it really confusing that people could treat field as an integer type.

168
00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:46.260
Jonathan Sobel: Because it seemed to me that it was this special… I went and I asked people, what is field? People ask people treating it as a number. Oh, it represents this thing, and they described something to me that sounded like an opaque thing.

169
00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:52.369
Jonathan Sobel: And so, it has actually always felt weird to me that it is part of the kind of implicit integer hierarchy.

170
00:19:52.470 --> 00:19:54.600
Jonathan Sobel: Just as a newcomer to it.

171
00:19:55.530 --> 00:20:00.519
Jonathan Sobel: That feels like an accident of implementation, not an expected part of the semantics.

172
00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:13.520
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, Cass said… Cass used exactly the same terminology when I was describing the situation to him, that it was an accident of the representation rather than something deliberate. I…

173
00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:23.549
Kevin Millikin: I think I… I think I believe that as well. I have been spending a day and a half, almost two days now, updating all of our tests to add field casts.

174
00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:29.350
Kevin Millikin: Or to change field type to some big Uint type, just so that they type check.

175
00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:45.669
Kevin Millikin: And that's really an awkward change to make to existing code. Our test is probably the largest chunk of compact code that anyone in the world has, so it's… I'm acutely feeling the pain. I do think, though, that if I were writing that code in the first place, it would be fine.

176
00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:55.949
Kevin Millikin: And in some ways beneficial. Our recommendation has always been, like, you should steer clear of field arithmetic. There's a big hazard that you have unintended wraparound.

177
00:20:56.430 --> 00:21:06.129
