345
00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:14.660
Thomas Kerber: And that there's… you have to find the right nuance of…

346
00:40:16.550 --> 00:40:25.189
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, this discussion essentially is the right one to have, because you need to define braking in such a way that you can still do things

347
00:40:25.360 --> 00:40:28.930
Thomas Kerber: Without everything ending up being considered breaking.

348
00:40:29.510 --> 00:40:37.599
Thomas Kerber: I think on… the other side is I've used plenty of languages that ostensibly follow Senver.

349
00:40:37.970 --> 00:40:43.500
Thomas Kerber: And are definitively not following it strictly, and are definitively introducing

350
00:40:43.740 --> 00:40:45.870
Thomas Kerber: Changes that are, to some extent, breaking.

351
00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:48.360
Thomas Kerber: I also think we… we have a…

352
00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:55.520
Thomas Kerber: Additional challenge, which is also a mitigating factor, namely that…

353
00:40:55.810 --> 00:41:00.779
Thomas Kerber: In a sense, any semantic change whatsoever is breaking.

354
00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:08.410
Thomas Kerber: Because if the circuits change for your contract, then… You…

355
00:41:08.700 --> 00:41:13.070
Thomas Kerber: Can't use that new version, if you've already deployed the previous one.

356
00:41:13.440 --> 00:41:28.690
Thomas Kerber: And what… what that means is we're kind of already locked into… because obviously we are going to change compiler outputs, we will have to. We're kind of already locked into, if you want to use a particular contract on-chain, you need to use

357
00:41:29.290 --> 00:41:32.640
Thomas Kerber: A specific version of the compiler to use that.

358
00:41:33.330 --> 00:41:39.609
Thomas Kerber: Which, to my mind, means there's actually more flexibility for

359
00:41:39.720 --> 00:41:43.640
Thomas Kerber: What kind of changes are allowed between compiler versions.

360
00:41:43.950 --> 00:41:47.599
Thomas Kerber: So I… I would err on the side of…

361
00:41:48.640 --> 00:41:54.989
Thomas Kerber: Not looking too closely on what is breaking, then, I'm focusing on it.

362
00:41:57.890 --> 00:42:02.160
Jonathan Sobel: Yeah, I'll, two things. First of all.

363
00:42:02.530 --> 00:42:11.019
Jonathan Sobel: it's sometimes useful to remember that SemVar, I think has kind of created thinking about libraries, and it's a little different to think about languages.

364
00:42:11.640 --> 00:42:24.110
Jonathan Sobel: And some of the stuff Thomas brought up fits that. And the other thing I was going to mention, sort of along with Kent's extreme examples of some of the things, is, having worked at SAS,

365
00:42:24.320 --> 00:42:26.839
Jonathan Sobel: They ran into this kind of thing all the time.

366
00:42:27.140 --> 00:42:34.819
Jonathan Sobel: And it was because it would be that, we're going to fix this bug, and it would turn out that someone was relying on that bug.

367
00:42:35.200 --> 00:42:44.419
Jonathan Sobel: And how do you actually… how strict do you keep it? What do you… what do you decide? And it really was a, it depends on the case-by-case basis.

368
00:42:44.690 --> 00:42:51.260
Jonathan Sobel: And so there were cases at SAS that they would say, oh, it turns out that the government of France

369
00:42:51.270 --> 00:43:04.569
Jonathan Sobel: has written code so that its entire national economy depends on this bug. We're gonna add a flag that says, run this new version, but preserve that bug behavior so that we don't bring down the nation of France when we…

370
00:43:04.570 --> 00:43:13.930
Jonathan Sobel: release our new version. And they would just kind of do these sorts of surveys. I don't remember if it's France, but I'm… that was actually true for some country in real life.

371
00:43:14.100 --> 00:43:31.159
Jonathan Sobel: And so they would kind of get a sense, when they had these questions, of how many people care, and they would reach out there and reach out to the community, and sometimes they would be convinced that no one cared, and they would go ahead and make the change, and it turned out it was a big deal, and they'd quick release a patch to preserve the bug behavior.

372
00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:51.319
Kent Dybvig: I think that preserving, backward compatibility is more about source…

373
00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:55.670
Kent Dybvig: Not having to edit your source than it is about

374
00:43:55.820 --> 00:44:01.590
Kent Dybvig: Being able to, you know, getting the same binary out in languages.

375
00:44:08.740 --> 00:44:16.320
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, but I think, like, one of the very common exceptions I've seen is I don't think any,

376
00:44:16.710 --> 00:44:23.810
Thomas Kerber: language I have used considers adding new names, or… Sorry, I…

377
00:44:23.960 --> 00:44:30.260
Thomas Kerber: Adding new functions to a standard library, or adding new modules, or this kind of thing, but breaking change.

378
00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:35.259
Kent Dybvig: Some languages, it's not an issue, because there isn't actually

379
00:44:35.420 --> 00:44:40.250
Kent Dybvig: a conflict, right? Like, in C, you don't… you won't resolve to the standard level.

380
00:44:40.250 --> 00:44:40.570
Thomas Kerber: Exactly.

381
00:44:40.570 --> 00:44:42.370
Kent Dybvig: If you have a local definition.

382
00:44:42.600 --> 00:44:49.799
Kent Dybvig: And so the standard library adds something, and it can't conflict with yours, in general.

383
00:44:49.800 --> 00:44:54.170
Thomas Kerber: That is true. But for languages where it can cause a conflict.

384
00:44:55.040 --> 00:44:57.580
Thomas Kerber: It's usually just an accepted risk.

385
00:44:58.140 --> 00:45:02.320
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, and I will say that, I made a decision

386
00:45:02.530 --> 00:45:08.819
Kent Dybvig: early on in Chase Games development, around year 15 or so, the backward compatibility was not,

387
00:45:09.280 --> 00:45:13.090
Kent Dybvig: Was not an aspiration at all, because…

388
00:45:13.360 --> 00:45:21.099
Kent Dybvig: as I think I wrote in one of… one of… in the History of Shays Scheme paper, that I… I think I wrote, you know.

389
00:45:21.460 --> 00:45:25.900
Kent Dybvig: It doesn't make sense in a system whose lifetime is measured in decades.

390
00:45:26.500 --> 00:45:27.170
Kent Dybvig: It…

391
00:45:27.660 --> 00:45:39.879
Kent Dybvig: It really hampers you from, you know… you really can't have a better language 20 years from now if you're… if you're having to maintain full compatibility with everything you could do 20 years ago.

392
00:45:40.680 --> 00:45:59.159
Kent Dybvig: Languages improve, language technology, ideas come, and old things fall away, and that's as it should be. And if you are a slave to backward compatibility, your language will necessarily die after a relatively short period of time relative to

393
00:45:59.460 --> 00:46:05.550
Kent Dybvig: Chase schema, which is, what, 40… some years now.

394
00:46:06.830 --> 00:46:20.919
Jonathan Sobel: Yeah, I'll agree with your assertion that this was… that source compatibility is the win. I mean, the one system… here's another anecdote, the one system I rely on this kind of annotation, what version on… I'm on, all the time is Lilypond. I write music.

395
00:46:21.490 --> 00:46:40.830
Jonathan Sobel: And Lilypond, I can put at the top, this is for this version of Lilypond. And it's so convenient when they release new versions with slightly different syntax, to just be able to build my old files and know that it will, at least from moderately recent ones, it will just do the right thing. It will go, oh, that was for this prior version, that means you interpret this this way.

396
00:46:40.830 --> 00:46:46.470
Jonathan Sobel: And I also know that I don't… it doesn't work back arbitrarily far to, like, version 1.0 or something.

397
00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:52.330
Jonathan Sobel: But it's really nice, right, when a new version comes out with some little change, not to have to go edit my code right away.

398
00:46:56.410 --> 00:46:59.470
Thomas Kerber: Just, just thinking out loud,

399
00:47:01.050 --> 00:47:08.629
Thomas Kerber: two examples of, like, that would come to mind, one which I don't think worked well, so that one is, Python.

400
00:47:09.020 --> 00:47:15.790
Thomas Kerber: Where they tried to support backwards compatibility for a long time, and then make one big braking change.

401
00:47:16.140 --> 00:47:17.010
Thomas Kerber: And…

402
00:47:17.440 --> 00:47:23.560
Thomas Kerber: As a result, they kind of got the worst of both worlds, where they had a lot of packages that

403
00:47:24.260 --> 00:47:27.450
Thomas Kerber: Because of the backwards compatibility, did not

404
00:47:27.910 --> 00:47:31.540
Thomas Kerber: Regularly need to update, and therefore didn't update.

405
00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:33.960
Thomas Kerber: When it was actually required.

406
00:47:34.920 --> 00:47:39.260
Thomas Kerber: And I think the other one is the Rust ecosystem.

407
00:47:39.820 --> 00:47:45.170
Thomas Kerber: Which… Is kind of dealing with backwards compatibility by

408
00:47:45.870 --> 00:47:52.259
Thomas Kerber: shifting it, and I think that's a bit closer to what Jonathan was saying, that it's kind of more on the…

409
00:47:52.940 --> 00:47:55.070
Thomas Kerber: source level.

410
00:47:55.500 --> 00:48:00.400
Thomas Kerber: In that you can specify as part of the… package,

411
00:48:00.930 --> 00:48:06.790
Thomas Kerber: containing. You can say, oh, I'm targeting this language version. And the neat thing about that

412
00:48:07.340 --> 00:48:14.129
Thomas Kerber: I'm talking about additions in Rust in case you want to look into it. The neat thing about that is that

413
00:48:14.470 --> 00:48:21.869
Thomas Kerber: you can interoperate between them. So it's… it's a source-level thing, but they all share the same backend, so…

414
00:48:24.690 --> 00:48:32.169
Thomas Kerber: I don't know how feasible that is for our case. I don't know if it even makes sense before something like contract-to-contract calls.

415
00:48:32.340 --> 00:48:39.540
Thomas Kerber: But I found that to be a neat solution for, making changes to an otherwise stable language.

416
00:48:47.910 --> 00:48:58.289
Andrzej Kopeć: Yeah, which makes me want to also mention, another form of a backward compatibility, and forward compatibility as well, actually.

417
00:48:58.700 --> 00:49:11.849
Andrzej Kopeć: Which is, called binary compatibility, especially in the Java ecosystem, where… The artifacts are published, compiled.

418
00:49:11.970 --> 00:49:29.629
Andrzej Kopeć: Already, and in that case, it is very important to ensure that someone linking against published artifacts will be able to link against the newer version of them, as long as that new version is claimed to be compatible.

419
00:49:29.630 --> 00:49:33.139
Andrzej Kopeć: Which means that, roughly speaking, the ABI should be the same.

420
00:49:33.300 --> 00:49:34.130
Andrzej Kopeć: And…

421
00:49:34.540 --> 00:49:48.850
Andrzej Kopeć: In this context, it is particularly important because of the ZKR and how it interacts and how it relates to the verifier and prover keys.

422
00:49:51.880 --> 00:49:55.290
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, so, just to… Answered.

423
00:49:55.400 --> 00:49:58.929
Thomas Kerber: Or to give some perspective on that.

424
00:50:00.320 --> 00:50:09.349
Thomas Kerber: at the moment, the plan, and I don't know if we have committed to that. If we have not, I should commit to it. But the plan is to

425
00:50:10.120 --> 00:50:17.480
Thomas Kerber: to preserve that kind of binary compatibility. So, in particular, the ZKR format has a version field.

426
00:50:17.730 --> 00:50:24.969
Thomas Kerber: And I'm looking to guarantee that the same source file will always produce the same output.

427
00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:27.790
Thomas Kerber: By utilizing that version field.

428
00:50:27.920 --> 00:50:36.290
Thomas Kerber: So you will… if you… target CKR version 2.0, you will always generate the same prover verifier keys.

429
00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:45.339
Thomas Kerber: And yeah, that's something that I would like to support going forward, and that does come with quite a bit of…

430
00:50:45.520 --> 00:50:56.680
Thomas Kerber: additional effort on the ZKR side, but I see that as a requirement to actually be able to interact with old contracts, because we're not going… you know, this is essentially the…

431
00:50:57.090 --> 00:51:00.589
Thomas Kerber: The binaries that have been deployed, and we're not going to get to rewrite those.

